## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical streaming analytics under fixed memory, focusing on real-time percentile estimation and how rebinding affects accuracy and cost.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms, fixed memory, boundary recomputation, percentile estimation, amortized analysis.
- Trade-offs between K, R, and accuracy; cache-friendly bucket access.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Histogram85 {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M; this.K = K;
    this.bounds = Array.from({length: K}, (_,i)=> Math.floor((i+1)*M/K));
    this.counts = new Array(K).fill(0);
  }
  update(x){
    let bucket = Math.min(this.K-1, Math.floor(x/ (this.M/this.K)));
    this.counts[bucket]++;
  }
  total(){ return this.counts.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0); }
  percentile(p){ // p in [0,1]
    const total = this.total();
    const target = p * total;
    let acc = 0;
    for(let i=this.K-1; i>=0; i--){
      acc += this.counts[i];
      if(acc >= target) return this.bounds[i-1] !== undefined ? this.bounds[i-1] : this.M;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose K and R for skewed data?
- How does this scale with M and batch size B?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming cardinality with bounded memory and concrete trade-offs between accuracy, memory, and speed.

## Key Concepts
- Cardinality estimation in streams
- HyperLogLog algorithm and error scaling
- Trade-offs: memory vs. accuracy vs. per-item cost

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m) {
    this.m = m; // must be power of two
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }
  add(item) {
    const x = BigInt("0x" + sha1(item));
    const idx = Number((x >> 64n) & BigInt(this.m - 1));
    const w = Number((x & ((1n << 64n) - 1n)) | 0n);
    const rank = this._rho(w);
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], rank);
  }
  _rho(v) {
    let r = 1;
    while (((v >> (64 - r)) & 1) === 0 && r <= 64) r++;
    return r;
  }
  estimate() {
    // simplified estimate; actual implementation uses harmonic mean with bias corrections
    const Z = Array.from(this.registers).reduce((a,b)=>a+Math.pow(2,-b),0);
    const alpha = this.m ? 0.7213/(1+1.079/this.m) : 0;
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / Z;
  }
}
```

